# Code Quality

Read first: `architecture-principles.mdc` and `project-structure.mdc`; this rule only covers tooling, hygiene, and CI gates.

## Ruff (0.15.12+)

- Selected rules: `E,F,I,B,UP,SIM,N,RUF,ASYNC,S,PL,PT,TID,TCH`.
- `--fix` via `make format` locally; CI runs `ruff check` + `ruff format --check`.
- Use `# noqa: <code>` only with a one-line justification comment.

## Black (26.3.1+)

- Line length **100**.
- Target Python 3.14: `target-version = ["py314"]`.
- Never disable Black on a file; reformat instead.

## mypy (1.20.2+) — strict

- `strict = true`; `warn_redundant_casts = true`; `warn_unused_ignores = true`.
- No `Any` without a `# type: ignore[<code>]` comment with reason, or an explicit `cast(...)`.
- Use the SQLAlchemy plugin (`plugins = ["sqlalchemy.ext.mypy.plugin"]`) and the Pydantic mypy plugin where helpful.

## Git hooks (host stubs + GNU `make`, no `pre-commit` package)

- **Source:** `docker/.githooks/pre-commit` and `docker/.githooks/pre-push` (tracked in the repo).
- **Install:** `scripts/install_git_hooks.sh` copies templates → `.git/hooks/` (copy-only). **`make setup ENV=local|test`** invokes it best-effort; **`make install-git-hooks`** (`precommit-install`) does the same copy with required success (**`make install`** separately builds the dev image for lint/tests).
- **Commit hook:** runs **`make git-hooks-commit`** (lint + **`make check-env`**).
- **Pre-push hook:** runs **`make git-hooks-push`** (same as **`make ci-local`**: lint, audit, check-env, i18n-check, test), or **`GIT_HOOKS_PUSH_QUICK=1`** for **`git-hooks-push-quick`** (no pytest).

## Security scanning (CI gates)

- `pip-audit` — fail on **High** severity CVEs.
- `bandit -r app` — fail on `HIGH` confidence + `HIGH` severity.
- `detect-secrets scan --baseline .secrets.baseline` — no new findings.

## Conventional Commits

- `feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `test:`, `perf:`, `build:`, `ci:`.
- Subject ≤ 72 chars, imperative mood. PR title is enforced as a Conventional Commit.
- Squash-merge to `main`.

## Docstrings

- Google-style. Required on public APIs of services, repositories, and `app/core/*`.
- Private helpers don't need docs but need clear names.

## PR hygiene

- One concern per PR. Migrations + tests live in the same PR as the model change.
- PR description includes: change summary, motivation, migration note, rollback plan, observability impact.
- Update `.env.example` when adding env vars (CI drift check fails otherwise).
- **Gettext catalogs** when adding user-visible strings. Use the workflow in `i18n-l10n.mdc`; CI runs `make i18n-check`.

## Banned in PRs

- Commented-out code (git remembers).
- TODOs without a linked ticket ID.
- Print statements (use structlog).
- `Any` without a justification comment.
- Hardcoded user-visible strings (use `_("...")` / `Translator`).
- f-string formatting of user-visible numbers / dates / currencies (use `Translator.format_*`).
- `if locale == "ar":` (or any locale) branches in business code.

## Review gates

- Two AI review passes before merge; branch protection still requires at least one human approval per spec §16:
- **Code review** (architecture, DI, Pydantic, SQLAlchemy, async, typing, i18n, testing): see `code-review.mdc`. Run via `/review-code` (Claude Code) or invoke `@code-review` rule in Cursor.
- **Security review** (authn/z, secrets, SQLi/SSRF/IDOR, CORS, rate limit, containers, supply chain, audit logging, PII): see `security-review.mdc`. Run via `/review-security` or `@security-review`.

CI gates (mypy, ruff, black, pytest, pip-audit, bandit, detect-secrets, Trivy/image scan, `make i18n-check` — all via **Docker** in Bitbucket) are necessary but not sufficient — the reviewer rubrics catch design and exploitability issues that linters can't.
